export const START_HERE_LESSON_MD: string = "## Who this lesson is for\n\nIf you're new to AI, or have tried it a few times and wondered how to get more from it, start here. If you already use AI most days, some of these tips will be familiar. You can skim the headings and choose a habit to practise in the project.\n\nThe full course is for anyone from a complete beginner to a confident user. The first lessons make sure everyone has the same foundations. Later lessons cover building tools, working with data and automating everyday work.\n\n## Why this lesson comes first\n\nMany of us started by typing long questions and reading every answer on screen. We lost track of requests that worked. We paid for subscriptions before we knew what each tool was good at.\n\nThese are the habits we wish we'd known at the start. Several take only a few minutes to set up. They can save you time in later lessons and help you remember what you learn.\n\nFor today, start with three things: ask one question using dictation, save how you like your answers, and create a learning notebook. The project walks you through them with Handy, a free dictation app, as your voice key. It also helps you save a reusable prompt and check your privacy setting. A prompt is a question or instruction you give AI.\n\nThe seven main tips explain these habits and help you choose a tool. The ten shorter habits draw on research, guidance from AI companies and experts. The shorter habits point you to the relevant lesson in Month 1 of the course, or a step in this project. You don't need to try everything today.\n\nBefore trying any activity, keep personal and confidential information out of a personal AI account, including screenshots and uploaded documents. Use fictional examples or remove private details. For work, use your organisation's approved tool and follow its policy.\n\n## What you will be able to do by the end\n\n- Speak to AI instead of typing, and listen to long answers and lessons instead of reading them.\n- Ask AI plain questions and improve its answers by replying with what you need changed.\n- Ask AI to write a better request, or prompt, for you, without first learning special techniques for writing one.\n- Use examples to show AI what you want, instead of trying to describe it.\n- Keep a learning notebook for your notes, ideas and prompts that worked.\n- Start with free or low-cost plans and decide when paying more is worthwhile.\n- Use the GWTH labs to compare different versions of AI, called models, on the same real-life scenarios. The labs show how each model does, helping you choose one that fits your needs and save money.\n- Find the later Month 1 lessons on checking answers and protecting private information, and the project steps for checking work and testing saved instructions.\n\n## Tip 1: Speak to AI and listen when it helps\n\nWhen you have a lot to say, speaking may be quicker than typing. Most of us speak much faster than we type. OpenAI makes the same point in its guidance for people using ChatGPT at work.\n\nThere are three ways to speak to AI or listen to text:\n\n- **Dictation.** You speak and it types your words into the box. You can read and correct them before you press send.\n- **Voice mode.** You have a spoken conversation. You talk, and the AI talks back.\n- **Read aloud.** The AI, or your browser, reads an answer or a page to you.\n\n### Use dictation in any AI tool\n\nTry the dictation built into your computer or phone. Click or tap in an AI message box or email first. Speak a short sentence and check that your words appear there.\n\n- **Windows:** press the Windows key and H together, then speak. It types into whatever box your cursor is in. It needs an internet connection.\n- **Mac:** turn on Dictation in System Settings, under Keyboard. Then press the microphone key, or use the dictation shortcut, and speak.\n- **iPhone and Android:** tap the microphone on the keyboard.\n\nChatGPT, Claude and Gemini also have their own microphone buttons in the message box. Check whether the button types into the message box or starts a spoken conversation.\n\nDictation can mishear names, place names and technical words. Read the text before you send it, as you would check a text message.\n\nIn Month 1 of the course, you'll build your own dictation tool with its own dictionary of your words. It should get better at recognising your voice the more you use it.\n\nYou don't need to speak in tidy sentences. Talk the way you would explain the problem to a colleague: \"I've got a letter from the council about a parking permit, I don't understand the second paragraph, can you explain it in plain English?\" AI copes well with the \"ums\" and second thoughts of normal speech.\n\n### Use voice mode to think out loud\n\nUse voice mode to think something through, practise a difficult conversation or ask questions while you're walking or cooking. ChatGPT Voice, Claude's voice mode and Gemini Live in the Gemini phone app are available on free plans, with limits.\n\n### Listen instead of reading\n\nIf reading long answers is tiring, look for a speaker icon or an option called Read aloud or Listen beside the reply, including when you're using a free plan. In ChatGPT, you may need to open the reply's three-dot menu; in Gemini, look for Listen, as described in [Google's voice help](https://support.google.com/gemini/answer/15277943?hl=en). [OpenAI's app help](https://help.openai.com/en/articles/8142208-chatgpt-ios-and-android-app-faq) explains ChatGPT's reply controls.\n\n![Example of ChatGPT's Read aloud control](https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/xGYWnszb4FftgMQoG5nyGc.jpg)\n\nExample screenshot: Future, via [Tom's Guide](https://www.tomsguide.com/ai/chatgpt/chatgpt-can-now-read-out-loud-heres-how-i-use-it-instead-of-audible-or-speechify); the position of the control may differ on your device.\n\nIf your tool doesn't show a listening option, Microsoft Edge can read web pages and PDFs aloud using Ctrl, Shift and U together, with a choice of voice and speed. Google's NotebookLM can also turn documents you upload into a spoken summary you can listen to like a podcast.\n\nEvery GWTH lesson has audio too, so you can listen while you walk if that suits you.\n\n## Tip 2: Ask AI as you would ask a new colleague\n\nYou can speak to AI in ordinary language. According to the AI companies' own guidance, a clear goal is the main thing it needs.\n\nGive AI the information you would give a capable new colleague. It can't know what you mean by \"the usual format\" or what matters to your organisation unless you tell it. Anthropic, the company that makes Claude, suggests imagining how a colleague with no knowledge of the task would read your request. If that person would need more detail, add it for the AI too.\n\nWhen you ask AI for help:\n\n- **Use plain words.** \"Can you explain what a P60 is?\" is a good prompt.\n- **Start with the goal.** Say what the result is for: \"I need an email that helps my team prepare for our office move.\" Then explain who will read it and what a useful result would include. If you give the AI a role, keep it to one line to guide the tone: \"Write as a helpful colleague.\" For a bigger job, first ask: \"What would a top professional do to approach this task?\" Read its suggested approach, then ask it to work that way.\n- **Include details you might take for granted.** If you ask for party food ideas, the AI can't know that two of your guests are vegetarian unless you tell it.\n- **Reply to its answer.** Treat the first answer as a draft. Say what you want changed: \"Shorter, please.\" \"Make it warmer.\" \"My manager is new, so explain the acronyms.\"\n\nReplying to the first answer matters most. If it's bland, say what needs to change. You may get a better result after two or three replies.\n\nYou can also ask AI to show where it needs more information:\n\n- **Ask it to list its assumptions.** Before a bigger piece of work, ask: \"Before you start, list the assumptions you are making.\" Correct any that are wrong, then let it carry on.\n- **Ask anything you need to understand.** If an answer is too complicated, ask AI to explain it more plainly, as many times as you need. You can also ask how to use the tool, such as where to find a setting or what a word means. It may not know about features released in the last few months. If its directions don't match what you see, check the tool's help pages.\n\nIf you return to the same saved chat, the AI can use its earlier messages. If you start a new chat, it may not have yesterday's details. Some tools have a memory feature that carries selected details between chats. Repeat anything important in your request. You'll also need to check the facts in its answers, as you'll see below.\n\nLater in Month 1 you'll learn a fuller way to brief AI for bigger jobs. For now, begin with plain questions and reply when an answer needs work.\n\n## Tip 3: Ask AI to write a prompt for you\n\nYou can ask AI to improve a prompt, including a prompt you plan to give that same AI. You don't have to learn special wording first. Anthropic and OpenAI both build tools for developers that do this. OpenAI staff also list \"ask it to improve your prompt\" among their everyday tips.\n\n**Let it interview you.** Start with your rough request, then add:\n\n> Before you answer, ask me any questions you need to do this well. Ask them one at a time.\n\nThe AI will ask about your audience, your purpose and anything else it needs. Answering its questions is often quicker than trying to include every detail in your first request.\n\n**Let it improve your prompt.** Paste in what you were going to ask, then add:\n\n> Rewrite this as a better prompt. Then tell me what you changed and why.\n\nRead the explanation to see which details it added and why. Check that the new prompt still asks for your goal. Correct any detail the AI has assumed.\n\nWhen the result is good, copy the prompt into your learning notebook (Tip 5) so you can use it again.\n\nFor small jobs, the questions can be more trouble than they're worth. If the AI starts asking about something simple, say \"Skip the questions and just have a go.\" You can always correct it afterwards.\n\n## Tip 4: Show it an example whenever you can\n\nAn example shows AI the format, tone or structure you want. Anthropic, the company that makes Claude, says examples are one of the most reliable ways to guide those parts of an answer.\n\n### Give an example of what you want\n\nIf you want an email, report or social media post in a particular style, remove its private details before pasting it in. You could use a fictional example such as \"Please remember that our team meeting starts at ten. Bring one idea you'd like us to discuss.\" Then say what you want the AI to copy:\n\n> Here is an email I wrote last month that went down well with my team. Write the new one about the office move in the same tone and at about the same length.\n\nOne example helps. Two or three examples give the AI a better sense of what they have in common. With only one, it may follow it too closely.\n\n### Give an example of what you do not want\n\nYou can also show an answer you don't want. Tell the AI what's wrong with it:\n\n> Here is the kind of answer I do not want. It is too formal and too long, and it uses jargon my readers will not know.\n\n### Share the document when you can\n\nIf you want the AI to use your company's style guide, your CV or a job advert, remove anything private first. Upload or paste the whole document rather than choosing a few paragraphs. Tell the AI what you need the document for, such as preparing for an interview. The full document gives it details that your summary may miss.\n\n### Ask AI to give you an example\n\nWhen an explanation doesn't make sense, ask the AI for an example:\n\n> Can you give me an example of that from a job in HR in the UK?\n\nAn example from your own work may make the explanation clearer.\n\nWhen an example gets great results, save it in your learning notebook next to the prompt that used it. It might be a model email or a well-written summary. Good examples are as worth keeping as good prompts.\n\n## Tip 5: Keep a learning notebook\n\nOver the next three months you'll try many prompts, tools and ideas. Keep a notebook so you can find what worked and build on what you've learned.\n\n### What to keep\n\nGive your notebook these four headings:\n\n- **Prompts that worked.** Copy in the exact wording, any example you gave it, and a line about what it was for.\n- **What went wrong.** Note wrong answers, made-up facts and tools that didn't do what you hoped. These notes save you repeating a mistake.\n- **Ideas.** Things you'd like to try with AI at work or at home.\n- **Questions.** Anything you want to come back to later in the course.\n\nYou can return to \"Prompts that worked\" whenever a similar task comes up.\n\n### Where to keep it\n\nYou can keep your notebook inside the course. On every GWTH lesson page, you can write notes next to the part you're reading. On the My notes page, you'll find your notes from every lesson in one place.\n\nYour notes belong to you. You can download all of them at any time as a single file that Notion, Obsidian and most other notes apps can open. If you later choose another tool, you can take your notes with you.\n\nIf you'd rather use your own tool from the start, choose one you'll keep using:\n\n- **Notion.** Popular, flexible and free for personal use. A good choice if you already use it.\n- **Obsidian.** Free, including for work, and needs no account. Your notes are ordinary files saved on your own computer, so you control where they're kept.\n- **OneNote or a Word document.** Both work well, especially if your workplace already uses Microsoft 365.\n- **Joplin or Anytype.** Free options that keep your notes on your own device, if privacy matters most to you.\n\n### Keep related AI chats together\n\nYou can also keep related chats and files together inside some AI tools.\n\nMost AI tools have a **projects** feature that keeps chats, files and instructions for one piece of work together. You could set up a \"GWTH course\" project.\n\n**ChatGPT Space** is a different feature, launched by OpenAI in September 2026. It's a shared workspace where teams and AI work on documents together. At launch, it was available only on the higher-priced Pro plan and the business plans. You don't need it for this course.\n\nEven if you use a project in an AI tool, keep your learning notebook too. You can take your notes with you if you change tools. Writing things down in your own words also helps you remember them.\n\n## Tip 6: Start small and start cheap\n\nAn **AI tool** is the app you use, such as ChatGPT. A **plan** is the free or paid package that sets which features you can use and how much you can use them. A **model** is the version of AI answering inside the tool. Choosing a model is different from buying a plan.\n\nYou can begin learning on a free plan. The free plans of the main AI tools include chat, voice, web search and file uploads. Paid plans mainly let you use the tool more before you reach a limit. They also give you access to the strongest models, which are the most capable versions of the AI.\n\n### When to use a paid plan\n\n1. **Start free.** Use the free version of one or two tools for your first couple of weeks.\n2. **Consider one paid plan if you keep hitting limits** or need a feature your free plan doesn't include. Check important answers on any plan.\n3. **Pay for one plan at a time while you're learning.** Avoid paying for two subscriptions that do the same job.\n\nHere are the main plans. Always check the provider's own pricing page before you pay.\n\n| Tool | Free plan | Lower-cost plan | Main paid plan |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ChatGPT | Yes, with adverts in the UK | Go, under £10 a month, also with adverts | Plus, around £20 a month |\n| Claude | Yes | None | Pro, about £20 a month including VAT |\n| Google Gemini | Yes | Google AI Plus, £4.49 a month | Google AI Pro, £18.99 a month |\n| Meta AI | Yes, inside WhatsApp, Instagram and Facebook | None in the UK yet | None in the UK yet |\n| Grok | Yes, also inside X | SuperGrok Lite, £10 a month | SuperGrok, £30 a month |\n| Perplexity | Yes | None | Pro, £20 a month |\n| DeepSeek (China) | Yes, free | None | None |\n\nDeepSeek stores what you type in China, so keep anything personal or work-related out of it.\n\nIf your workplace gives you Microsoft Copilot, most of the tips in this lesson work there too.\n\n### Use smaller models for small jobs\n\nMost AI tools offer a faster, cheaper model alongside their most powerful one. If your account lets you choose, read the descriptions in its model menu to distinguish the faster option from the more powerful one. Check the tool's help pages if you can't find the menu. If you have no choice, use the model provided. You don't need to pay just to follow this lesson.\n\nFor tidying an email, summarising a page or suggesting a subject line, try the faster model first. It's often good enough and uses less of your allowance. Try the most powerful model, or a \"think harder\" setting, when the stakes are high or the first answer isn't good enough.\n\nEthan Mollick, a Wharton professor who writes widely about AI at work, says free models are fine when the stakes are low. He says paying for a good model is worthwhile once you use AI for real work. Let your own use guide when you pay.\n\nLater in Month 1 of the course, in a lesson called Your AI Garage, you'll test free and low-cost models properly. You'll also learn how to keep your costs down as you use AI more.\n\n## Tip 7: Look at the labs before you choose\n\nClaims about the \"best\" AI model tell you little about how it'll handle your own tasks. A model can do well at one task and poorly at a similar one. Researchers at Harvard and the Boston Consulting Group call this the \"jagged frontier\". The first full lesson in Month 1 of the course explains it in more detail.\n\nThe GWTH labs compare different AI models on the same real-life UK scenarios, such as writing a job advert or tidying a messy spreadsheet. Each lab shows the models' answers side by side. It scores them against the same checklist and gives a dated verdict.\n\nThe labs show how each model performs, so you can save money by picking the model that best fits your needs.\n\nOpen the GWTH labs from the course page. Read two or three labs about tasks that interest you. For each one, ask:\n\n- Which answer would I rather send?\n- Which model asked better questions, or made fewer mistakes?\n- Which one sounds more like the way I write?\n\nNote your answers in your learning notebook to help you choose a model to start with. You can change tools later. In the course, you'll also test tools on your own tasks.\n\n## Ten more habits worth starting now\n\n1. Use AI daily on a real task you'd otherwise put off, starting with the plain questions in Tip 2 of this Month 1 lesson.\n2. Check anything that matters, with practice in the new Month 1 lesson on AI safety.\n3. Keep personal and confidential information out of personal AI accounts, with more guidance in the new Month 1 lesson on AI safety.\n4. Ask for several options so you can compare them, using the plain requests taught in Tip 2 of this Month 1 lesson.\n5. Ask AI to challenge your ideas in a fresh chat, with more guidance in Month 1 lesson L03, How AI Works, under \"AI agrees with you too easily\".\n6. Rehearse a difficult conversation out loud, with interview practice in Month 1 lesson L15.\n7. Break big jobs into steps you can check as you go, using the seven steps taught in Month 1 lesson L08.\n8. Read and edit AI-written text before sending it, using examples to show the voice you want as taught in Tip 4 of this Month 1 lesson.\n9. Save how you like your answers, following Step 2 of this project. Whenever the model changes, try the same task with and without those instructions. Compare the answers before deciding what to keep. Saved guidance can go stale. This also applies to reusable task instructions called skills and files of background information called context files.\n10. Get to know one AI tool before adding another habit or feature, starting with Tip 6 of this Month 1 lesson.\n\n## Where to learn more in Month 1\n\nIn Month 1, lesson L03, How AI Works, explains how to keep each chat focused. It introduces the context window, which is the amount of information AI can use at once. Lesson L06 teaches when to give AI the whole document. Lesson L20, Your AI Garage, explains in detail when to use a subscription and when to use an API. An API lets software send requests directly to an AI service instead of using its chat app. Lesson L02, Your AI Colleague, teaches a fuller brief for bigger jobs. The new Month 1 lesson on AI safety teaches the thirty-second fact check, and lesson L05, Frontier Labs Tooling, covers a monthly check of the main AI tools. Month 1 lesson L13, Agent Safety, teaches how to stay in control when AI takes actions for you.\n\n## Getting the most from the course\n\nChoose a regular time to read or listen, then do the project with your notebook open and add notes as you go. Ask AI for another explanation if something doesn't make sense. Every lesson has short, straightforward questions spaced through it to help you check what you remember as you go; they aren't there to trick you. Return to the GWTH labs to compare different models on the same real-life scenarios and choose one that fits your needs, which can save you money as tools change.\n\n## Your project: set up your AI starter kit\n\nAllow about 25 minutes for the starter kit, plus time to install Handy and download its speech model. This is the file Handy uses to turn your speech into text. The checking practice can take longer, so you can finish the extra review chats later. The project gives you these steps:\n\n1. Set up the dictation app Handy with a keyboard shortcut. It types what you say into the selected text box. Use it to ask an AI one real question.\n2. Save instructions that tell your AI tool how you like your answers, and compare its answers with and without them.\n3. Create your learning notebook with the four headings from Tip 5.\n4. Ask an AI to interview you about a real task, starting with what you want the result to achieve. Then ask it to write a reusable prompt for that task and save the prompt in your notebook.\n5. Never ask the chat that made something to judge its own work. Start a fresh chat, give it the work and ask it to find what is wrong. For work that matters, ask two or three fresh chats to criticise it independently. Give their comments to another fresh chat and ask it to separate fair points from weak ones. Decide for yourself which changes to make. Use a separate chat to find a primary source for every number and factual claim. A primary source is the original evidence, such as an official statistics release or the research paper reporting a study. Open the sources your decision depends on and check that they support the answer.\n6. Check your privacy setting in the AI tool you use most.\n\n## Recap\n\n- Start with one free tool and use the labs to choose a model that fits your tasks and budget.\n- Speak instead of typing, or listen instead of reading, when it helps; check dictated text before sending it.\n- Ask plain questions, reply with what needs changing, and show examples of what you want.\n- Let AI help you write a prompt, then check it still asks for your goal and save it in your notebook.\n- Set up Handy, your answer preferences and your learning notebook in the project, which also guides you through checking work and your privacy setting.\n- Keep personal and confidential information out of personal AI accounts; the later Month 1 lessons above give you more practice with checking, safety and bigger tasks.\n\n## Next: Welcome to GWTH\n\nIn Month 1 of the course, in a lesson called Welcome to GWTH, you'll meet the six ways AI can help you. You'll also see what you'll be able to build over the three months. Keep your notebook open to add what you learn.\n"

export const START_HERE_PROJECT_MD: string = "# Student Project: Your AI Starter Kit With a Voice Key\n\n## Why this matters\n\nAllow about 25 minutes for the starter kit, plus time to install Handy and download its speech model. The checking practice in Step 5 may take longer. You can return to its extra review chats later.\n\nYou'll set up three things for the lessons that follow. The first is a voice key: a keyboard shortcut that types what you say into any box on your computer. The second is a short set of saved instructions that tells your AI tool how you like your answers. The third is a learning notebook for the prompts that work for you. A prompt is the instruction or question you give an AI.\n\nYou'll also practise two checking habits. You'll ask fresh chats to find what is wrong with a piece of work. You'll then ask a separate chat to find a primary source for each fact. A primary source gives information directly from the organisation or person responsible for it. For example, use HMRC's own guidance to check a claim about tax.\n\nYou can pause after setting up your voice key, testing your answer instructions, saving a reusable prompt in your notebook and recording your privacy choice. Note any unfinished checking practice under **Questions**, then return to Step 5 to finish it. The aim is to make these lesson tips habits you can keep using.\n\n## Before you start: protect private information\n\n- Use a personal task for this project, or a work task that contains no personal or confidential information.\n- Do not paste anything about a named person, such as a customer, patient, pupil, colleague or family member, into a personal AI account.\n- If you want to try this with a work task, use the AI tool your organisation has approved. Follow its policy.\n\nIf you wouldn't post it on a public noticeboard, don't paste it into a personal AI account.\n\n## What you need\n\n- A Windows computer or a Mac with a microphone. A phone or tablet works too, using the route at the end of Step 1.\n- A free account with at least one AI tool, such as ChatGPT, Claude or Gemini. A second tool is useful for Step 5 but not essential.\n- About 25 minutes for setup and a first practice, plus installation and extra checking time. The step timings below are guides, not deadlines.\n\n## Step 1: Set up your voice key with Handy (about 6 minutes, plus installation)\n\nHandy is a free dictation app for Windows and Mac. You press a shortcut and speak. It types your words into whatever box your cursor is in: an AI chat, an email or a document. It turns your speech into text on your own computer, so your voice isn't sent to the internet. Once you send the text to an AI tool, that tool's privacy rules apply. Handy's [setup guide](https://handy.computer/docs) explains installation and microphone permissions.\n\n1. Go to [handy.computer](https://handy.computer) and download the version for your computer. Open the downloaded file from your browser's downloads or your Downloads folder. On Windows, follow the installer's prompts, then open Handy. On a Mac, if the download opens a window showing Handy and Applications, drag Handy into Applications, then open it there. Follow any setup instructions shown by the download.\n2. Open Handy. It will ask to download a speech model, which is the part that recognises your words. Choose the one it suggests. The download can be several hundred megabytes or more, depending on the model, so it may take a few minutes. Check the size shown before downloading. Handy's [model guide](https://handy.computer/docs/models) lists the options. Wait until it finishes and Handy shows a selected speech model rather than download progress.\n3. On a Mac, Handy will also ask for permission to use the microphone and for Accessibility access. Accessibility access lets it type into other apps. Allow both. If you miss a request, open System Settings, then Privacy & Security. Turn Handy on under Microphone and under Accessibility.\n4. Open your AI tool and click in the message box. Keep Handy open. Check the shortcut displayed in Handy's settings before trying it.\n5. The default shortcut is **Ctrl and Space** on Windows, or **Option and Space** on a Mac. Use the shortcut shown in your settings if you've changed it. In push-to-talk mode, Handy records while you hold the shortcut. Keep holding the keys while you ask one real question out loud about something you'd like to know this week. For example: \"I've been asked to give a five-minute talk at work about a project I finished. Can you suggest a simple structure?\"\n6. Release the keys to stop recording. If Handy keeps recording after you release them, press the shortcut again to stop. You can change the shortcut in Handy's settings. You can also choose whether to hold the keys or press once to start and again to stop.\n7. You should see your words in the message box. Read them, correct any mistakes, then press send.\n\nIf nothing appears, click in the message box again. Check that Handy has a downloaded model, that your microphone is selected and allowed in your computer's privacy settings, and that you're using the shortcut shown in Handy. On a Mac, check Accessibility access too. Try one short sentence. If setup still stalls, use the built-in route below and return to Handy later.\n\n**If you can't install apps on this computer or Handy setup stalls,** for example on a work laptop, use the dictation already built in:\n\n- **Windows:** click in the message box, then press the Windows key and H together. This needs an internet connection.\n- **Mac:** go to System Settings, then Keyboard, and turn on Dictation. Use the shortcut shown there.\n- **iPhone or Android:** tap the microphone on the keyboard.\n\n**Listen to the answer too.** Look for a speaker icon or a Read aloud or Listen option beside the reply. In ChatGPT, check the reply's three-dot menu too; [OpenAI's app help](https://help.openai.com/en/articles/8142208-chatgpt-ios-and-android-app-faq) explains the controls. In Gemini, look for Listen, as described in [Google's voice help](https://support.google.com/gemini/answer/15277943?hl=en).\n\nIf your AI tool doesn't have a read-aloud option, open the chat in Microsoft Edge and choose Read aloud from its menu. The shortcut is Ctrl, Shift and U on Windows, or Command, Shift and U on a Mac.\n\n**Check:** your spoken words appeared in the message box, you corrected them and sent the question. When you choose Read aloud, you should hear the reply; check your volume if you hear nothing.\n\n## Step 2: Save how you like your answers (4 minutes)\n\nMost AI tools let you save instructions for every new chat, so you don't have to repeat them. You'll compare the same question with and without your answer preferences. An AI model is the system within your AI tool that produces the answers. Use the same tool and model for both answers. Change only the instructions.\n\n1. Find the box for saved instructions in the AI tool you use most:\n   - **ChatGPT:** Settings, then Personalization, then Custom instructions. On a phone, look for Customize ChatGPT in Settings. See [OpenAI's instructions help](https://help.openai.com/en/articles/8096356-chatgpt-custom-instructions).\n   - **Claude:** click your initials, then Settings, then Your Claude. Enter your preferences under Instructions. In the older layout, look under Account for Instructions for Claude. See [Claude's personalisation help](https://support.claude.com/en/articles/10185728-understanding-claude-s-personalization-features).\n   - **Gemini:** open Settings, then Personal Intelligence, then Instructions for Gemini. See [Google's instructions help](https://support.google.com/gemini/answer/16598625?hl=en).\n\n   Menus change. If you can't find the setting, check your tool's help page for saved instructions. Some accounts do not offer this feature. If yours does not, save the instructions in your learning notebook in Step 3. Paste them at the start of each new chat, then ask your question.\n\n2. Check whether the box already contains instructions. Copy them into a temporary note so you can restore them. Switch the instructions off, or temporarily remove them and save. In a new chat, ask your Step 1 question to get an answer without them. If there were no instructions in Step 1, you can use that answer instead. If there is no saved-instructions feature, ask the question alone in a new chat.\n\n3. Use your voice key to say these instructions into the box, or type them. If your account has no saved-instructions setting, enter them at the start of your chat instead. Change any line so that it suits you:\n\n   > Give me the answer first, in plain English, then the detail. Keep answers short unless I ask for more. If you are not sure about something, say so. Ask me a question if my request is unclear.\n\n4. Save the instructions if your tool offers that setting. Switch them back on if you turned them off. Start a new chat and ask your question from Step 1 again. If you can't save instructions in your tool, paste them before your question. Compare the answers against the instructions you used. For the suggested instructions, check whether the new answer gives the answer first and keeps it short. No visible improvement is a valid result. Keep only instructions that help, and restore any useful original instructions from your copy.\n\n5. Make this a habit. Saved instructions are written for the model you have now. When your AI tool gets a new model, ask the same question with your instructions switched off and then on. Compare the answers again. Keep the lines that still help and delete the rest. Write \"Retest my instructions when the model changes\" under **Questions** in your notebook in Step 3.\n\n**Check:** you have used your instructions in a new chat and compared the answers with and without them.\n\n## Step 3: Create your learning notebook (3 minutes)\n\n1. Use a notes tool you already know. If you have Word, open it, choose a blank document and save it as **GWTH learning notebook** in a folder you can find again. You can also create a page with that name in Notion, Obsidian, OneNote or another familiar tool.\n2. If you prefer course notes, you can write next to the part of this lesson you're reading and find your notes together on **My notes**. You can download all your course notes later. If you can't find or reopen those notes, use the document route for now.\n3. Add these four headings:\n   - Prompts that worked\n   - What went wrong\n   - Ideas\n   - Questions\n4. Under **Prompts that worked**, paste the instructions you used in Step 2. You can then reuse them in another AI tool or paste them at the start of each new chat. Under **Questions**, add the reminder from Step 2: \"Retest my instructions when the model changes.\"\n5. Under **Ideas**, write one thing you'd like AI to help you with by the end of the course. Try saying it with your voice key.\n\n**Check:** save your notebook, close it and reopen it from the folder you chose, or from My notes. Confirm that all four headings, your instructions, the reminder to retest them and your idea are still there.\n\n## Step 4: Let AI write a prompt with you (4 minutes)\n\n1. Choose a real task you will do in the next week, such as an email, a short plan or a summary of something you have read.\n2. Use your voice key to describe the task to your AI tool in a sentence or two. Start with the goal: say what you want the result to achieve. For example: \"I want my manager to agree to a three-month trial of working from home on Fridays.\" You do not need to give the AI a role such as \"You are an expert\". If the task is about a document, take out names, addresses and other private details. Then paste or upload the whole document rather than picking out the paragraphs you think matter. Add:\n\n   > Before you answer, ask me any questions you need to do this well. Ask them one at a time.\n\n3. Answer its questions, using your voice key if that is quicker. After a few questions, or once you've supplied the important details, say: \"Use what I've told you to write a first draft now. Mark anything you still need to know rather than inventing it.\"\n4. Read the draft. Check that it serves your goal and hasn't invented details. Ask for corrections if needed. Then ask:\n\n   > Write a prompt for a similar task next time. Start it with the goal, and leave clear spaces for the details I will need to change.\n\n5. Check that the reusable prompt keeps your goal and leaves spaces for details that will change. For example:\n\n   > Goal: I want my manager to agree to [trial length] of working from home on [day]. Task: draft an email. Context: [reasons and practical arrangements]. Format: a short email. Limits: use only the details I give you; ask about anything missing.\n\n6. Copy your checked prompt into **Prompts that worked**. Add a line explaining its purpose and keep any example you supplied beside it.\n\n**Check:** you've saved one prompt that starts with the goal and that you could reuse next week.\n\n## Step 5: Check the work in fresh chats (allow more than 6 minutes if needed)\n\nA chat tends to favour the work it has produced. Use new chats for these two checks. You can start with one criticism chat and the source check today, then return to finish the second critic and sorting chat. Use the full process for work that matters.\n\n**Find what is wrong.**\n\n1. Open a new chat, in a different AI tool if you have one. Paste in your task description and the result from Step 4. If the task used a document, include that too, with private details removed. Then ask:\n\n   > Find everything that's wrong with this. Give me the strongest case against it.\n\n2. Do the same in a second new chat. If you're stopping after one critic today, return here later before opening the sorting chat.\n3. Once you have both reviews, open a third new chat to sort them. Paste in your task description, the result from Step 4 and both lists of criticisms. If the task used a document, include the whole document too, with private details removed. Then ask:\n\n   > Which of these points are fair, and which are weak?\n\n4. Check each criticism against your original task or document. For the working-from-home example, \"The draft doesn't say the trial lasts three months\" is fair if your request specified that and the draft missed it. \"Your manager will refuse Friday working\" is unsupported unless you supplied evidence for it. The sorting chat can help, but it cannot decide for you.\n5. Under **What went wrong**, record a change and the evidence for it, or write that no change was needed. Explain any criticisms you rejected.\n\n**Find a primary source for each fact.**\n\n1. Ask your AI tool one factual question you care about, for example: \"What does a P60 show, and when should my employer give me one?\"\n2. Open a new chat, paste in the answer and ask:\n\n   > Find a primary source for every number and claim in this, and check that each one is a good primary source. List anything you can't find. Give me working links and the passage supporting each claim.\n\n3. Open every primary source your decision depends on, such as relevant GOV.UK pages. Read the supporting passages yourself and compare their names, numbers and dates with the answer. If one official page supports every claim you need, record that.\n4. If a link fails, search the named organisation's own website for the claim. If you can't open a source or match the claim to its words, record the claim as **unverified** under **What went wrong**. Don't rely on it for a decision. Record mismatches there too.\n\n**Check:** two fresh chats have criticised your work, a third has sorted their points, and you've decided what to change using your task or document as evidence. You've also opened every primary source your decision depends on. Mark any unsupported claim as unverified. If you've paused after the first critic, note the remaining chats under **Questions** and return to finish them.\n\n## Step 6: Check your privacy setting (2 minutes)\n\nIf you only use an account provided by your organisation, check its policy on how chats are used. Record what you find in your notebook.\n\nIf you use a personal AI account, find the setting that controls whether your chats are used to train future models:\n\n- **ChatGPT:** Settings, then Data controls. Look for **Improve the model for everyone**. See [OpenAI's data controls help](https://help.openai.com/en/articles/7730893-data-controls-in-chatgpt).\n- **Claude:** Settings, then Privacy. Look for **Help Improve our AI models**. See [Anthropic's model improvement settings help](https://privacy.claude.com/en/articles/12109829-how-do-i-change-my-model-improvement-privacy-settings).\n- **Gemini:** open Gemini Apps Activity and look for **Keep Activity**. See [Google's activity settings help](https://support.google.com/gemini/answer/13278892?hl=en) and its [privacy explanation](https://support.google.com/gemini/answer/13594961?hl=en).\n\nRead the explanation beside the setting before choosing on or off. Turning off training is a sensible default, but it does not mean chats cannot be stored. Gemini's activity control also concerns saved activity; read Google's explanation rather than assuming all three tools work identically.\n\nIf the menu differs, use the linked official help. You can ask the AI, \"Where do I find the setting that controls whether my chats are used for training?\", but compare its directions with that help if they don't match your screen.\n\nUnder **Questions**, record the setting's name and whether you chose on or off. Reopen the setting to confirm its state. If you can't find or change it, record that uncertainty rather than assuming training is off.\n\n**Check:** you have recorded the setting and state you actually saw, or your organisation's policy. Any unresolved setting is clearly marked for follow-up.\n\n## What you have prepared\n\n- A voice key that types what you say into any box on your computer.\n- Instructions saved in your AI tool or your notebook, tested with and without, and a note to retest them when the model changes.\n- A learning notebook with four headings and your first saved prompt, which starts with the goal.\n- A way to check work in fresh chats: critics, a sorting chat and a primary-source check. If you paused, your notebook records what remains to practise.\n- A privacy setting you have checked and chosen, or notes on your organisation's policy.\n\nIf you have five more minutes, open the GWTH Labs page from the course menu. Read one lab comparing different AI models on the same real-life scenario. See how each performs on a task like yours; choosing the model that fits your needs can save you money. Later in Month 1 of the course, you'll build your own private dictation tool with its own dictionary of your words, so it should get better at recognising your voice as you use it. In this project, you're setting up Handy.\n\nKeep the notebook open in the next lesson and add to it as you go.\n"
